    $ts$2025-10-14T09:00:00+07:00$ts$::timestamptz,
    'published',
    $s$investment-flows-beyond-java$s$,
    $t$Investment flows shift beyond Java as regional hubs accelerate.$t$,
    $e$More than half of realized investment in H1 2026 landed outside Jakarta — changing how national campaigns need to be planned and localized.$e$,
    $st$Investment flows shift beyond Java as regional hubs accelerate$st$,
    $sd$More than half of realized investment in H1 2026 landed outside Jakarta — changing how national campaigns need to be planned and localized.$sd$,
    $c$Market$c$,
    $img$https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1486406146926-c627a92ad1ab?w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80$img$,
    $alt$Modern city skyline at dusk$alt$,
    $an$Sari Wijaya$an$,
    $ar$Editor, Markets$ar$,
    $av$https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1573496359142-b8d87734a5a2?w=96&h=96&fit=crop&q=80$av$,
    $body$<p>For years, a “national” campaign in Indonesia often meant Jakarta first, then a light pass at the rest of the country. The H1 2026 investment numbers make that habit expensive. More than half of realized investment landed outside Jakarta, and half of the national total sat outside Java altogether.</p><p>That shift is already showing up in coverage requests. Brands entering Surabaya, Medan, Makassar, and Bali want the same proof they used to reserve for the capital: local outlets, local language, and a story that does not read like a Jakarta release with the city name swapped.</p><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1486406146926-c627a92ad1ab?w=1400&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;q=80" alt="Modern city skyline at dusk"><h2>What changes in the brief</h2><p>A single national wire run still matters for search and headquarters reporting. It is no longer enough if the audience, the capital, and the next office are in a different province. City and region targeting has to be in the plan from the start, not added after the Jakarta placements go live.</p><ul><li><strong>Stop treating “outside Java” as one bucket.</strong> Each hub has its own outlets, creator tiers, and editorial habits.</li><li><strong>Localize before the first draft.</strong> The campaigns that land map those differences in the newsroom, not after publish.</li><li><strong>Keep the national run.</strong> Use it for search and reporting — then add the city mix the investment map now requires.</li></ul><h2>Looking ahead</h2><p>Teams that still write one Jakarta story and sprinkle city names will miss the readers — and the capital — that have already moved. Plan the mix the way the investment is already flowing.</p>$body$
  ),
(
    $ts$2025-09-18T09:00:00+07:00$ts$::timestamptz,
    $ts$2025-09-18T09:00:00+07:00$ts$::timestamptz,
    $ts$2025-09-18T09:00:00+07:00$ts$::timestamptz,
    'published',
    $s$editors-tighten-ai-pitches$s$,
    $t$Editors tighten standards as AI-generated pitches flood inboxes.$t$,
    $e$Newsrooms report a spike in generic releases. Teams that lead with verified facts, quotes, and Indonesia-specific context are still getting picked up.$e$,
    $st$Editors tighten standards as AI-generated pitches flood inboxes$st$,
    $sd$Newsrooms report a spike in generic releases. Teams that lead with verified facts, quotes, and Indonesia-specific context are still getting picked up.$sd$,
    $c$Media$c$,
    $img$https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1504711434969-e33886168f5c?w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80$img$,
    $alt$Newspapers and reading glasses on a desk$alt$,
    $an$Daniel Hartono$an$,
    $ar$Media correspondent$ar$,
    $av$https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1507003211169-0a1dd7228f2d?w=96&h=96&fit=crop&q=80$av$,
    $body$<p>Editors across Indonesian newsrooms are seeing the same inbox: a stack of releases that read polished, interchangeable, and slightly off. The spike tracks with generic AI drafts — copy that names a product, skips the reporter’s beat, and could have been written for any market.</p><p>The filter is getting stricter, not more automatic. Desks still pick up stories that lead with a verified number, a named source, and a reason the story matters here.</p><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1504711434969-e33886168f5c?w=1400&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;q=80" alt="Newspapers and reading glasses on a desk"><h2>What still gets picked up</h2><ul><li><strong>A verified fact in the first sentence.</strong> A quote from an Indonesia lead, a city, a regulator, or a customer on the ground still beats global boilerplate.</li><li><strong>A reason it matters here.</strong> If the only localization is a translated headline, most editors will pass.</li><li><strong>A draft that was actually edited.</strong> The bar is not anti-tool. It is anti-generic.</li></ul><h2>What is dying</h2><p>The “announce and spray” brief. Editors have seen that version twenty times this week, and they can tell when nobody checked the facts against a local outlet’s standards. Teams that hit send on the first pass are training desks to ignore the next one.</p>$body$
  ),
(
    $ts$2025-08-21T09:00:00+07:00$ts$::timestamptz,
    $ts$2025-08-21T09:00:00+07:00$ts$::timestamptz,
    $ts$2025-08-21T09:00:00+07:00$ts$::timestamptz,
    'published',
    $s$creator-clips-press-line$s$,
    $t$Creator clips extend the half-life of a single press line.$t$,
    $e$Short-form explainers and regional creators are turning one wire story into multi-day conversation — if the angle fits the platform.$e$,
    $st$Creator clips extend the half-life of a single press line$st$,
    $sd$Short-form explainers and regional creators are turning one wire story into multi-day conversation — if the angle fits the platform.$sd$,
    $c$Distribution$c$,
    $img$https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1611162616305-c69b3fa7fbe0?w=1400&auto=format&fit=crop&q=80$img$,
    $alt$Person recording video on a smartphone$alt$,
    $an$Maya Putri$an$,
    $ar$Distribution editor$ar$,
    $av$https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1580489944761-15a19d654956?w=96&h=96&fit=crop&q=80$av$,
    $body$<p>A press line used to have a one-day life: it published, it sat in a report, and the conversation moved on. Creator clips are stretching that window. One wire story, recut as a short explainer or a regional take, can keep the same fact in circulation for days.</p><p>The catch is the angle. A release written for a business desk does not automatically work as a TikTok or Reels script. Creators need a hook, a visual, and a reason their audience should care — not a pasted executive quote.</p><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1611162616305-c69b3fa7fbe0?w=1400&amp;auto=format&amp;fit=crop&amp;q=80" alt="Person recording video on a smartphone"><h2>How to brief both from the start</h2><ul><li><strong>Treat the wire story as the source, not the finished distribution.</strong> Publish it, keep the canonical URL live, then let creators extend the line.</li><li><strong>Prefer regional voices over one national face.</strong> A clip that lands in Bandung or Makassar often does more for local trust than another pass at the same Jakarta account.</li><li><strong>Match the city you actually need.</strong> Paying for reach that does not match the launch city is how budgets disappear.</li></ul><h2>What the report should show</h2><p>Placements and the clips that kept the story alive. If the report only lists outlet logos, you have not captured the half-life you paid for.</p>$body$
  ),
(
    $ts$2025-07-09T09:00:00+07:00$ts$::timestamptz,
